+To be sure, a caught rocket is not a utopia. The Culture runs on physics we don't have and on machine benevolence nobody knows how to engineer; Banks could simply decree that his Minds were kind. Musk's calendar is famously elastic: he promised a million robotaxis by 2020 and cargo ships on Mars by 2022, and both dates came and went. To some, there is an awkward fact at the center of his enthusiasm: the Culture has no billionaires. Banks himself asked how readers who called his books a libertarian ideal had missed that his paradise abolished private property and money. Musk, though, has not missed it. He predicts that, with enough AI and robotics, money "will stop being relevant." In a civilization so rich that money has withered away, nobody is a billionaire, and nobody needs to be.
 
 These objections strengthen the case for reading the blueprint. Musk is reliably late, yet on his two biggest bets, electric cars and reusable rockets, he was right about the direction while his critics were wrong. Nothing in this argument requires the Culture to arrive on schedule, or at all. It requires only that the industrialist with the most power to shape the coming decades has named his destination and just demonstrated the vehicle. And each objection is really a question the novels force into the open. Who owns the abundance? Who answers for the Minds? What are people for when nothing needs doing? Better to argue over those now, while the answers are still open, than to meet them later as terms of service.
 
